        425.8025817871094,
        82.31007385253906,
        438.2259521484375,
        91.54541015625
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "where",
      "bbox": [
        42.710723876953125,
        108.09626770019531,
        67.59730529785156,
        117.33160400390625
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "ˆpθ.yn|y1:n−1/= 1",
      "bbox": [
        175.6536407470703,
        123.15766906738281,
        249.42062377929688,
        141.22982788085938
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "N",
      "bbox": [
        242.82029724121094,
        137.5537109375,
        250.30221557617188,
        144.34820556640625
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "N\u0003",
      "bbox": [
        255.12820434570312,
        121.83259582519531,
        265.6487121582031,
        131.79519653320312
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "k=1",
      "bbox": [
        253.6739959716797,
        140.68392944335938,
        267.09844970703125,
        148.3388214111328
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "wn.Xk",
      "bbox": [
        268.5375061035156,
        127.99638366699219,
        294.6382141113281,
        139.30702209472656
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "1:n/",
      "bbox": [
        291.1053161621094,
        130.48016357421875,
        305.2835693359375,
        141.7501983642578
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "is an estimate computed at time n of",
      "bbox": [
        42.711273193359375,
        155.0409698486328,
        194.59310913085938,
        164.27630615234375
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "pθ.yn|y1:n−1/=",
      "bbox": [
        104.02210235595703,
        175.75123596191406,
        168.60818481445312,
        186.96791076660156
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "\u0004",
      "bbox": [
        169.9919891357422,
        162.1911163330078,
        175.53118896484375,
        172.15371704101562
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "wn.x1:n/ qθ.xn|yn,xn−1/ pθ.x1:n−1|y1:n−1/dx1:n:",
      "bbox": [
        180.8950653076172,
        175.75123596191406,
        376.9175109863281,
        186.96791076660156
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "It follows from equation (7) that wn.x1:n/ only depends on x1:n through xn−1:n. We have omitted",
      "bbox": [
        42.711151123046875,
        200.12754821777344,
        438.2294616699219,
        211.01181030273438
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "the dependence on N in equations (5), (8) and (9), and will do so in the remainder of this section",
      "bbox": [
        42.71124267578125,
        212.08265686035156,
        438.23126220703125,
        221.3179931640625
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "when confusion is not possible.",
      "bbox": [
        42.710243225097656,
        224.0377655029297,
        173.4793243408203,
        233.27310180664062
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "2.2.2.",
      "bbox": [
        42.710243225097656,
        253.92555236816406,
        65.75373840332031,
        263.0911560058594
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Design issues and limitations",
      "bbox": [
        75.71633911132812,
        253.92555236816406,
        194.1217803955078,
        263.0911560058594
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "This algorithm requires us to specify qθ.x1|y1/ and {qθ.xn|yn,xn−1/;n=2,. . . ,T}. Guidelines on",
      "bbox": [
        42.710243225097656,
        265.1437072753906,
        438.22943115234375,
        276.3603820800781
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "howbesttoselect{qθ.xn|yn, xn−1/}arewellknown.Withpθ.xn|yn,xn−1/∝fθ.xn|xn−1/ gθ.yn|xn/,",
      "bbox": [
        42.710235595703125,
        277.09881591796875,
        439.496826171875,
        288.3157958984375
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "it is usually recommended to set qθ.xn|yn, xn−1/ = pθ.xn|yn,xn−1/ whenever possible and to",
      "bbox": [
        42.711395263671875,
        289.0541076660156,
        438.23089599609375,
        300.2707824707031
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "select qθ.xn|yn,xn−1/ as close as possible to pθ.xn|yn,xn−1/ otherwise; see for example Carpen-",
      "bbox": [
        42.711700439453125,
        301.0093078613281,
        438.2274169921875,
        312.2259826660156
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "ter et al. (1999), Cappé et al. (2005), Doucet and Johansen (2009), Liu (2001) and Pitt and",
      "bbox": [
        42.71022033691406,
        313.8013000488281,
        438.2263488769531,
        323.03662109375
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Shephard (1999). It is often much simpler to design these ‘local’ importance densities than",
      "bbox": [
        42.711212158203125,
        325.75640869140625,
        438.2243347167969,
        334.9917297363281
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "to design a global importance density approximating pθ.x1:T |y1:T /. An ‘extreme’ case, which",
      "bbox": [
        42.711212158203125,
        336.8747863769531,
        438.2305908203125,
        348.4864196777344
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "was originally suggested in Gordon et al. (1993), consists of using the prior density of the",
      "bbox": [
        42.71044921875,
        349.6667785644531,
        438.2275695800781,
        358.902099609375
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "latent Markov process {Xn;n ⩾1} as an importance density; i.e. set qθ.x1|y1/ = μθ.x1/ and",
      "bbox": [
        42.71044921875,
        360.7850036621094,
        438.2287902832031,
        372.0019836425781
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "qθ.xn|yn,xn−1/=fθ.xn|xn−1/. In scenarios where the observations are not too informative and",
      "bbox": [
        42.71160888671875,
        372.74029541015625,
        438.22723388671875,
        383.95697021484375
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "the dimension of the latent variable not too large, this default strategy can lead to satisfactory",
      "bbox": [
        42.710044860839844,
        385.53228759765625,
        438.2301330566406,
        394.7676086425781
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "performance. It is in fact the only possible practical choice for models where fθ.xn|xn−1/ is",
      "bbox": [
        42.710044860839844,
        396.65069580078125,
        438.2317199707031,
        407.86737060546875
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "intractable or too expensive to evaluate pointwise, but easy to sample from; see Ionides et al.",
      "bbox": [
        42.7115478515625,
        409.4427795410156,
        438.2317199707031,
        418.6781005859375
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "(2006) for many examples.",
      "bbox": [
        42.7115478515625,
        421.39788818359375,
        153.1071014404297,
        430.6332092285156
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Note that SMC methods also suffer from well-known drawbacks. Indeed, when T is too large,",
      "bbox": [
        52.67414855957031,
        433.3529968261719,
        438.230712890625,
        442.58831787109375
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "the SMC approximation to the joint density pθ.x1:T |y1:T / deteriorates as components sampled",
      "bbox": [
        42.71051025390625,
        444.47137451171875,
        438.2284851074219,
        456.0830078125
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "at any time n < T are not rejuvenated at subsequent time steps. As a result, when T −n is too",
      "bbox": [
        42.71034240722656,
        456.426513671875,
        438.23052978515625,
        466.49871826171875
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "large the approximation to the marginal pθ.xn|y1:T / is likely to be rather poor as the succes-",
      "bbox": [
        42.7103271484375,
        468.38189697265625,
        438.2278747558594,
        479.59857177734375
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "sive resampling steps deplete the number of distinct particle co-ordinates xn. This is the main",
      "bbox": [
        42.710693359375,
        481.1737976074219,
        438.2322082519531,
        490.4093017578125
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "reason behind the well-known difﬁculty of approximating p.θ,x1:T |y1:T / with SMC algorithms;",
      "bbox": [
        42.711029052734375,
        492.2920227050781,
        438.2297668457031,
        503.90380859375
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "see Andrieu et al. (1999), Fearnhead (2002) and Storvik (2002), for example. We shall see in",
      "bbox": [
        42.7105712890625,
        505.0840759277344,
        438.2305908203125,
        514.3193969726562
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "what follows that, in spite of its reliance on SMC methods as one of its components, PMCMC",
      "bbox": [
        42.7105712890625,
        517.0391845703125,
        438.2207946777344,
        526.2745361328125
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "sampling is much more robust and less likely to suffer from this depletion problem. This stems",
      "bbox": [
        42.7105712890625,
        528.9943237304688,
        438.2308349609375,
        538.2296752929688
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "from the fact that PMCMC methods do not require SMC algorithms to provide a reliable",
      "bbox": [
        42.7105712890625,
        540.949462890625,
        438.2277526855469,
        550.184814453125
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "approximation of pθ.x1:T |y1:T /, but only to return a single sample approximately distributed",
      "bbox": [
        42.7105712890625,
        552.068115234375,
        438.2295227050781,
        563.6795654296875
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "according to pθ.x1:T |y1:T /.",
      "bbox": [
        42.71031188964844,
        564.0230712890625,
        154.50680541992188,
        575.6347045898438
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "2.3.",
      "bbox": [
        42.71050262451172,
        594.7278442382812,
        59.328125,
        603.9432373046875
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Standard Markov chain Monte Carlo methods",
      "bbox": [
        68.6929702758789,
        594.7278442382812,
        268.7817687988281,
        603.9432373046875
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "A popular choice to sample from p.θ, x1:T |y1:T / with MCMC methods consists of alternately",
      "bbox": [
        42.71050262451172,
        605.9656372070312,
        438.2303466796875,
        617.5774536132812
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "updating the state components x1:T conditional on θ and θ conditional on x1:T . Sampling",
      "bbox": [
        42.711181640625,
        617.9210815429688,
        438.23052978515625,
        629.6422729492188
      ],
      "direction": [
        1.0,
        0.0
      ]
    }
  ],
